  "summary": "PASS for the standalone postconciliar homily deliverable of First Sunday of Advent, Year B (PC-S01-B, 2026-11-29; homily.tex [sha256 4d9165072a22], sections/homily/10-homily.tex [sha256 fc2feac78fde], sections/homily/90-note.tex [sha256 b5d8442ef55f], and research/production-review.md \u00a712). Read sections/homily/10-homily.tex continuously first as an adult parish listener, then verified all 14 direct Douay-Rheims study-English quotations verbatim against sections/20-appointed-texts.tex, cross-checked consistency with the accepted expansive and concise studies and sealed research records, and independently verified cited loci in held src/sources/ primary witnesses (Jerome In Isaiam XVII PL 24 cols. 642-651 raw OCR; Aquinas Super Isaiam ch. 63-64 Parma Vol. 14 raw OCR; Augustine Enarr. Ps. 79 \u00a7\u00a71, 3, 9 and Ps. 84 \u00a7\u00a76, 12; Chrysostom Hom. in 1 Cor. II \u00a7\u00a76-8; Hilary De Trinitate IX.67; Bede In Marcum IV.13 Wikisource/Corpus Corporum; Gregory Hom. 9 and 13; Aquinas Catena Aurea in Marcum 13:32-37 mediating Augustine Ep. 199 \u00a73; Cyril of Jerusalem Catechesis 15 \u00a7\u00a71, 4; Bellarmine Ps. 84 expl. 12; Sacramentarium Veronense XXVIIII.372). The homily passes the text-substitution test decisively: its governing movement turns directly on Isaiah 63:17's complaint and 64:1->3b Vg. (Heb. 63:19b->64:2b) wish-to-past tense shift, the Isa. 64:7 to Ps. 80(79):19 verbal reversal, Paul's Greek apokalupsis in 1 Cor. 1:7, Mark 13:33-37's departing householder and universal charge, and the Mass's prayer arc (Ps. 25[24]:1-3, Ps. 85[84]:8, Veronense XXVIIII.372, Ps. 85[84]:13), leading to precise Eucharistic theology and a single text-earned second-person response (Sacrament of Penance early in Advent) without excluded rhetorical devices, lecture-style authority stacking, invented anecdotes, clerical persona, or a newly composed prayer. Both iteration-2 findings are cleanly resolved: line 1 recasts the Gloria/Entrance opening as a tonal Advent contrast without implying liturgical substitution (HOM-008), and lines 21 and 23 explicitly identify the pre-Gospel acclamation as a psalm and anchor the Communion Antiphon to 'the psalm we heard before the Gospel' across the offering sentences (HOM-007). Spoken word count (1,478 spoken words; 1,474 raw source tokens across 14 paragraphs of 72-143 words; estimated 10.6-11.8 min at 125-140 wpm, ~11.4 min at 130 wpm) and 90-note.tex token count (1,376) match sections/homily/90-note.tex and research/production-review.md \u00a712 exactly. Meaningful limits honestly preserved without overstatement: delivery duration is a written word-count pace estimate rather than a timed human delivery; Douay-Rheims quotations remain public-domain study English rather than the copyrighted proclaimed U.S. Lectionary; protected modern Missal prayers remain withheld; Jerome, Aquinas (Super Isaiam), Bede (IV.13), Augustine (Ep. 199 \u00a73), and the secondary-2002 Missal comparisons carry their stated copy-quality and offline verification bounds; and the Jerome-Aquinas exegetical disagreement on Isa. 63:17 and 64:3b Vg. is transparently recorded in the terminal note."
